From db7f3212245c36b2f8fcbb3759d030def3913bd0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicola Nicodemo <nicola084@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:57:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: hamoa: throttle the CPUs at 95 C

The CPU and CPUSS thermal zones only have a critical trip at 115 C, so
Linux never limits the CPU clusters: under sustained load only the
firmware's own limits act. On the Galaxy Book4 Edge 14" (X1E80100), twenty
minutes of llama.cpp on all twelve cores (on the charger, with the 4.0 GHz
boost) held the CPU sensors at 108-112 C, with throughput falling to a
seventh while cpufreq still reported the boost frequency; the cpufreq
cooling devices stayed at state 0.

Give every CPU and CPUSS zone a passive trip at 95 C, polled every 200 ms
while passive, bound to its cluster's cpufreq cooling device, the same
values the GPU zones in this file use. The machine's ACPI tables (used by
Windows) set a passive trip at 105 C on the CPUs and GPU and lower ones on
board sensors.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ALoC6yerK2c2q13o4h7F4t
